<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aeschylus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 525–456 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aeschylus was an Athenian tragic playwright, often called the father of tragedy, who lived from about 525 to 456 BCE. He fought at Marathon and is credited with adding a second actor to the stage. Seven of his plays survive, including The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and the Oresteia trilogy. He visited the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-i-of-syracuse">Hieron I</a> of Syracuse and died at Gela in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeschylus.jpg" alt="Aeschylus" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zde · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herma_of_Aeschylus,_Klas08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athenian festivals produced tragedy and comedy: the surviving plays of <b>Aeschylus</b>, Sophocles, Euripides and Aristophanes remain the foundation of Western drama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Pliny says the Colchians were governed by their own kings in the earliest ages, and the Greeks knew the mythical Aeëtes, son of Helios and father of Medea, with his city Aea or Aia, before <b>Aeschylus</b> and Pindar first used Kolchis for a region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The odes of Pindar honoured victors at the sacred games, and the tragedies of <b>Aeschylus</b>, Sophocles and Euripides, performed at the festivals of Dionysus, explored the relations between gods and mortals; Euripides’ Bacchae dramatises the arrival of Dionysus and the punishment of those who refuse him.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The 5th and 4th centuries BCE, centred on Athens, produced the tragedies of <b>Aeschylus</b>, Sophocles and Euripides and the comedies of Aristophanes; history, invented as a form of inquiry by Herodotus and refined by Thucydides; the speeches of Demosthenes; and philosophy, from the dialogues of Plato to the…</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>In the century after Cleisthenes its citizens beat the Persians at the Battle of Marathon in 490 BCE, helped to defeat them at the Battle of Salamis in 480 BCE, built the Parthenon and staged the dramas of <b>Aeschylus</b>, Sophocles and Euripides.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>Gelon, master of Gela, took Syracuse in 485 BCE and with Theron of Acragas defeated a Carthaginian army at Himera in 480 BCE; his brother Hiero I of Syracuse welcomed Pindar, Bacchylides and <b>Aeschylus</b> to his court.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Elefsina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Gela, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.04,23.55&amp;year=-481" aria-label="Elefsina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5137 796 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aeschylus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5587 1066h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5587 1066h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.04,23.55&amp;year=-481">What was here in 481 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In visions of the night, like dropping rain, Descend the many memories of pain Before the spirit’s sight: through tears and dole Comes wisdom o’er the unwilling soul…&#8221;</p><footer>Agamemnon &#183; tr. E. D. A. Morshead, 1881 &#183; sung by the chorus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8604/pg8604-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;On, sons of Greece! On, for your country’s freedom! strike to save Wives, children, temples of ancestral gods, Graves of your fathers! now is all at stake.&#8221;</p><footer>The Persians &#183; tr. E. D. A. Morshead, 1908 &#183; the Greek battle cry at Salamis, as a Persian messenger reports it in the play &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8714/pg8714-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the inward vice of tyranny, To deem of friends as being secret foes.&#8221;</p><footer>Prometheus Bound &#183; tr. E. D. A. Morshead, 1908 &#183; spoken by Prometheus &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8714/pg8714-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
